Q: Why using a pointer of a self-defined structure in the CS50 Inheritance problem A: <p>In going back and reviewing this issue, I thought I would follow up with a sample program I wrote for another issue under this same theme.  First off, following is the code developed to address this issue and illustrate the usage of pointers to structures along with recursive calls to build, list, and free structures.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;time.h&gt;

// Each person has two parents and two alleles
typedef struct person
{
    struct person *parents[2];
    char alleles[2];
} person;

const int GENERATIONS   = 3;
const int INDENT_LENGTH = 4;

person *create_family(int);
void print_family(person *p, int);
void free_family(person *p);
char random_allele();

int main(void)
{
    // Seed random number generator
    srandom(time(0));

    // Create a new family with three generations
    person *p = create_family(GENERATIONS);

    // Print family tree of blood types
    print_family(p, 0);

    // Free memory
    free_family(p);

    return 0;
}

// Create a new individual with `generations`
person *create_family(int generations)
{
    // TODO: Allocate memory for new person
    person *p = malloc(sizeof(person));

    printf(&quot;Address of person of generation %d created is: %p\n&quot;, generations, p);

    // If there are still generations left to create
    if (generations &gt; 1)
    {
        // Create two new parents for current person by recursively calling create_family
        p-&gt;parents[0] = create_family(generations - 1);
        p-&gt;parents[1] = create_family(generations - 1);

        // TODO: Randomly assign current person's alleles based on the alleles of their parents
        p-&gt;alleles[0] = p-&gt;parents[0]-&gt;alleles[random() % 2];
        p-&gt;alleles[1] = p-&gt;parents[1]-&gt;alleles[random() % 2];
    }

    // If there are no generations left to create
    else
    {
        // TODO: Set parent pointers to NULL
        p-&gt;parents[0] = NULL;
        p-&gt;parents[1] = NULL;

        // TODO: Randomly assign alleles
        p-&gt;alleles[0] = random_allele();
        p-&gt;alleles[1] = random_allele();
    }

    // TODO: Return newly created person

    return p;
}

// Free `p` and all ancestors of `p`.
void free_family(person *p)
{
    // TODO: Handle base case
    if (p == NULL)
    {
        return;
    }
    // TODO: Free parents recursively
    free_family(p-&gt;parents[1]);
    free_family(p-&gt;parents[0]);
    // TODO: Free child

    printf(&quot;Address of person freed..: %p\n&quot;, p);

    free(p);

    return;
}

// Print each family member and their alleles.
void print_family(person *p, int generation)
{
    // Handle base case
    if (p == NULL)
    {
        return;
    }

    // Print indentation
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; generation * INDENT_LENGTH; i++)
    {
        printf(&quot; &quot;);
    }

    // Print person
    if (generation == 0)
    {
        printf(&quot;Child (Generation %d): blood type %c%c\n&quot;, generation, p-&gt;alleles[0], p-&gt;alleles[1]);
    }
    else if (generation == 1)
    {
        printf(&quot;Parent (Generation %d): blood type %c%c\n&quot;, generation, p-&gt;alleles[0], p-&gt;alleles[1]);
    }
    else
    {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; generation - 2; i++)
        {
            printf(&quot;Great-&quot;);
        }
        printf(&quot;Grandparent (Generation %d): blood type %c%c\n&quot;, generation, p-&gt;alleles[0], p-&gt;alleles[1]);
    }

    // Print parents of current generation
    print_family(p-&gt;parents[0], generation + 1);
    print_family(p-&gt;parents[1], generation + 1);

    return;
}

// Randomly chooses a blood type allele.
char random_allele()
{
    char abo[] = {'A', 'B', 'O'};

    return abo[random() % 3];
}
</code></pre>
<p>Kind of the key element to focus on in this code is the structure design itself.</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct person
{
    struct person *parents[2];
    char alleles[2];
} person;
</code></pre>
<p>Reviewing this design hints that this will be used in a type of linked list or tree, with recursive function calls to build the tree/list.  To build this tree/list with declared parent &quot;person&quot; structures, one would have to do the declaration, and then still assign the address of the declared structure to the parent pointers within the child structure; whereas, utilizing pointer definitions and allocating memory provides a more consistent approach as one works their way down the family tree.</p>
<p>With that following is a test execution of the code, with additional print statements added to illustrate the memory being allocated and freed.</p>
