<p>The main takeaways from this are an advocacy for code that utilizes customary practices for creation of variables, structures, and the like as well as striving for consistency in coding practices.  Consistency helps in future analysis of existing code.</p>

Q: Reading a line using scanf() not good? A: <blockquote>
<p>Reading a line using scanf() not good?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Using <code>scanf()</code> has a narrow advantage over <code>fgets()</code> when trying to read lines that <strong>may contain an embedded <em>null character</em></strong>, <code>'\0'</code>.<br />
Often this case is dismissed for <em>text</em> inputs, yet if needed, please continue.</p>
<p><code>fgets()</code> reads characters into a buffer.  If a <em>null character</em> is read, it is treated like any other non-<code>'\n'</code> character.  When <code>fgets()</code> is done, it appends a <em>null character</em> to the buffer.  Using <code>strlen()</code> later on does not then distinguish between a  read/saved <em>null character</em> and the appended one.  It reports the string <em>length</em> based on the first <em>null character</em> encountered.</p>
<p>With <code>scanf()</code>, we could use <code>&quot;%some_width[^\n]&quot;</code> to read most of the line and then <code>&quot;%n&quot;</code> to save the number of characters read.<br />
Let us assume a final <code>'\n'</code> is <strong>not</strong> saved in the buffer.<br />
I like to avoid hard-coding the <em>width</em>.<br />
Note: <code>&quot;%[^\n]&quot;</code> scanning immediately stops if the first character is a <code>'\n'</code>.  <code>buf[]</code> is not changed.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define STR_MAX_LENGTH 256
#define str(token) #token
#define xstr(token) str(token)

char buf[STR_MAX_LENGTH + 1];
int n;
int count = scanf(&quot;%&quot; xstr(STR_MAX_LENGTH) &quot;[^\n]%n&quot;, buf, &amp;n);
//                    ^   Width limit   ^  
</code></pre>
<p>Next we need to read the rest of the line, depending on <code>count</code> being <code>EOF</code>, <code>0</code> or <code>1</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>switch (count) {
  case EOF: break;  // We are done.
  case 0: {
    int ch = fgetc(stdin);
    assert(ch == '\n');
    buf[0] = 0;
    n = 0;
    break;
  }
  case 1: {
    int ch;
    bool buffer_too_small = false;
    while ((ch = fgetc(stdin)) != '\n') &amp;&amp; (ch != EOF)) {
      buffer_too_small = true;
    }
    if (ch == EOF &amp;&amp; !feof(stdin)) {  // Was an input error just detected?
      count = EOF;
    } else if (buffer_too_small) [
      TBD_Code();  // Silently ignore, increment n, ...?
    }
    break;
  }
}  

if (count == EOF) // No more input or rare input error.
else foo(buf, n);  // Use buf and n in some fashion
</code></pre>
<p>It is easy to assert that C lacks a good standard way to read a <em>line</em> with potential <em>null characters</em>.</p>
<p>IMO, C lacks a good standard way to read a <em>line</em> with or without potential <em>null characters</em>.</p>
